WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Squarespace.
You’ve probably seen one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.680
of their many YouTube sponsorships.
At one point, over 50% of revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.440
went back into marketing.
It feels like this brand was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.800
everywhere… but now, not so much.
So what happened?&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.720
This story is a bit different.
They didn’t have a rise and collapse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.560
like other companies, something&nbsp;
genuinely unexpected happened.&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.680
They went public… without&nbsp;
an IPO, and raised no money.&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.800
And, then they went private again?
This is how Squarespace exploded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:46.240
then went to war with… itself.
If you don’t know Squarespace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.640
it’s a very popular, no-coding website builder.&nbsp;
Pick a template catered to your needs, tweak it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.040
and boom. You have a website.
But the origin of Squarespace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.600
is a little bit different.
Way back in 2003, Anthony Casalena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.920
a computer science major, built the first&nbsp;
version from his University of Maryland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.240
dorm room, with a $30,000 loan from his parents.
[Anthony Casalena, Founder]: “I coded a blogging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.520
tool that I wanted to use. It wasn't until a&nbsp;
friend said he'd pay for it that I realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.280
I could also build a company.”
Pretty normal so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.440
but what isn’t, is how… sensible?
Over the next seven years, Squarespace grew to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:32.640
$10 million in annual revenue, and 30 staff, with&nbsp;
no funding. Aside from that first 30k, of course.&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.600
But, they had reached a ceiling. To scale&nbsp;
up more, to reach more people, invest into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.280
customer acquisition, they needed more money.
At this point, Getty Images tried to acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:49.360
them. This would have given Squarespace the&nbsp;
backing they needed, Casalena rejected the offer.&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.440
Instead, he was going a very&nbsp;
different route: VC funding.&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.040
Accel and Index Ventures were&nbsp;
brought in, investing $38.5 million.&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.760
But, this was a bit different.
[Anthony Casalena, Founder]: “This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.640
is a minority investment -- the same team&nbsp;
that has always managed Squarespace remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.840
in charge. This investment -- a growth stage&nbsp;
investment on top of an already successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.080
business -- will allow us to more rapidly&nbsp;
produce the sort of products we want to make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.040
and focus on getting those products to market.”
It’s quite often the case a startup begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.920
explode, needs capital, and the founder&nbsp;
gives up control, then things deteriorate.&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.920
With new funding, Squarespace&nbsp;
began to scale, and quick.&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:37.840
In 2015 they had over 500 employees, and by 2017,&nbsp;
they had over $300 million in revenue, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:45.440
crazily, was up 50% year over year. Insane growth.
But the question is, how?&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.880
Around this time, other website&nbsp;
builders were popping up.&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.320
What made Squarespace&nbsp;
different, and grow so quickly?&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.600
Unlike Wix and many other companies that tried&nbsp;
to capture a massive free market, and convert on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.520
that… Squarespace didn’t have a free tier.
No freemium model at all.&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.480
They positioned the brand around visual&nbsp;
quality, typography, “designer” templates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.960
and that “premium” experience. That&nbsp;
appealed strongly to photographers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.040
designers, restaurants, consultants, artists, etc.
Having to pay upfront reinforced this premium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.080
idea. This is also why they positioned&nbsp;
themselves as more a creative tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.240
than a regular website builder.
Campaigns used slogans like “build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.960
it beautiful”. Less technical, more artsy.&nbsp;
But for their core customers, a great idea.&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.080
Plus, no free tier meant they&nbsp;
didn’t have to provide support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.120
to customers who weren’t bringing in revenue.
Squarespace’s business model reinforced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.880
quality image. And, on that note, we need to&nbsp;
talk about Squarespace’s insane marketing.&nbsp;

