00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.760
Squarespace was putting a lot&nbsp;
of money back into marketing.&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.560
One of the biggest parts of&nbsp;
which was the Super Bowl.&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:41.680
Jeff Bridges in 2015, Key &amp; Peele in 2016,&nbsp;
Keanu Reeves in 2018, Zendaya + André 3000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:49.040
in 2022, Adam Driver in 2023, Martin&nbsp;
Scorsese in 2024, Emma Stone in 2026.&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.920
Not only are Super Bowl ads&nbsp;
expensive… but so are these actors.&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.680
But, it was quite important.
Squarespace wanted to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.884
a massive household brand.
[Anthony Casalena, Founder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.884 --> 00:06:02.240
CEO of Squarespace]: the same year:&nbsp;
"We want people to have heard of us."&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.920
They weren’t the only ones either, Wix&nbsp;
was doing the same thing, and a bit of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.520
advertising arms race began between the two.
And that continued through Sponsorships.&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.240
All over YouTube, and in particular,&nbsp;
podcasts, which were starting to boom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.600
Squarespace and Wix were there.
[Ryan Stansky, Squarespace Marketing]: “Podcasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.760
has been a base for us that we’ve scaled&nbsp;
up over the years. And as our marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.591
budget has grown each year, the podcast&nbsp;
budget has grown nearly proportionally.”&nbsp;

00:06:30.591 --> 00:06:31.280
[Anthony Casalena, Founder, CEO&nbsp;
of Squarespace]: “The audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.160
of people who listen to podcasts are well&nbsp;
educated, sophisticated, entrepreneurial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.200
and a really good audience for us.”
And, the results would speak for themselves.&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:47.040
So, if things were going so great, why did&nbsp;
they do an IPO, then delist the stock later?&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:52.800
Well, it turns out, they never did&nbsp;
an IPO… but something else entirely.&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:05.360
Which was smart, but came with a few risks.
By the end of financial year 2020, they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:12.800
$621 million in revenue, 60x growth over the&nbsp;
decade, and 50% compounding annual growth.&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:19.200
Subscriptions to Squarespace increased almost&nbsp;
23% in 2020 to 3.7 million. Keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.200
this was in the pandemic, and&nbsp;
everyone suddenly wanted a website.&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.560
Around this time, in April 2021,&nbsp;
Squarespace was preparing to go public.&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.640
But this is where things get a bit unusual.
Squarespace wasn’t doing an IPO.&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:41.520
However, they were going public on the NYSE.
But they wouldn’t be raising any money.&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.800
So… how? And why?
Isn’t the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:50.800
point of going public, to raise more money?
This is quite different to a lot of tech startups.&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.640
Squarespace filed for a direct listing of&nbsp;
shares. This means no new shares were created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:03.600
instead, existing shares were registered.
This was actually very smart, for a few reasons.&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:09.840
First, VC investors could now sell.
Accel and Index invested in July 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.920
Venture funds want to exit at some point, to make&nbsp;
a return, and they’d been around quite a while.&nbsp;

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.880
This lets them sell their&nbsp;
existing stock, if they like.&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.640
Plus, Squarespace didn’t really need the money.
They had just raised another $300 million a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.511
couple months earlier, which put&nbsp;
them at a $10 billion valuation.&nbsp;

00:08:28.511 --> 00:08:30.960
[Anthony Casalena, Founder, CEO of Squarespace]:&nbsp;
“A direct listing fit for us because Squarespace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.080
has been a profitable company for a number of&nbsp;
years and we don’t need to raise money in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.840
event. Our thinking was pursue the direct listing,&nbsp;
give people the option to buy if they want to buy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.160
sell if they want to sell. What’s great about&nbsp;
the direct listing is no one’s suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.240
unnecessary dilution today.”
Squarespace was making money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.960
had a strong roadmap, and had strong&nbsp;
growth… Why dilute their shares?&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.240
And this was the bigger reason.
No new shares, ownership doesn’t change.&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.160
Casalena didn’t want to dilute the shares&nbsp;
and give up more control. And rightly so.&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.920
There were 49,086,410 shares of&nbsp;
Class B stock for Squarespace, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:16.400
represented “68.2% of the total voting power”.
And all of this, was held by Anthony Casalena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:22.160
either directly or across multiple trusts.
After all the VC rounds and even going public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:28.080
the founder of Squarespace, was still in control.
Though, this public listing, didn’t quite go as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:33.840
planned, and brought an unexpected problem.
They were previously valued at $10 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:41.040
at $68.42 a share. This is when Tiger Global,&nbsp;
Dragoneer, D1, Fidelity, T. Rowe and Spruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.640
House invested in that $300 million raise.
But, this listing set Squarespace’s value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:53.840
at just $6.6 billion. It opened at just $48.
As it turns out, this listing, would create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:58.480
a massive point of tension between&nbsp;
Casalena, and the other shareholders…&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.560
And… why did they reverse course and&nbsp;
go private just a couple years later?&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.320
Well, immediately after&nbsp;
listing, some problems appeared.&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.880
Not only did the stock open below&nbsp;
expectations… it quickly tanked.&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.480
From $52 in May 2021, to just&nbsp;
$20 the same time next year.&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:18.880
Not great.
What happened?&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.800
To answer that, we first need to&nbsp;
look at some of their acquisitions.&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:29.040
In 2019 Squarespace bought Acuity Scheduling,&nbsp;
and then the social media content tool Unfold.&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:35.040
Then Tock in 2021, a restaurant reservation and&nbsp;
hospitality platform, for more than $400 million.&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.400
Then Google Domains in 2023, for $180 million
About 10 million domains, which would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:44.480
merged into Squarespace domains.
I can see the logic. Expand your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.440
offering and assets to make Squarespace a&nbsp;
more “all in one” package for businesses.&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.600
But, these acquisitions didn’t really&nbsp;
add value in the way Squarespace hoped.&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:59.440
Revenue was beginning to slow.
In Q2 2021, on revenue of $196 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:05.120
Squarespace had a net loss of $27.1&nbsp;
million, or $225.2 million if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:10.880
include a large write-down of Tock’s valuation.
Then for the 2022 financial year, an annual loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:19.200
of $252.2 million, on revenue of $867 million.
Then another net loss in 2023 of $7.1 million.&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:26.640
For all of 2022, revenue had grown just 10%. Not&nbsp;
terrible, but things were clearly slowing down.&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.400
So why did growth slow down?
And, why did Squarespace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:41.600
eventually leave the NYSE?
Did they delist? Or did something else happen?&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.520
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:50.080
means you won’t miss future videos.
I think Squarespace hit its ceiling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:52.320
just as Covid hit.
After the listing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.560
they were no longer benefiting from a fresh&nbsp;
wave of people discovering websites. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:01.760
competing for slower, more expensive growth in&nbsp;
a crowded and increasingly commoditised market.&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:07.200
The website market was maturing, and slowing down,&nbsp;
and it was becoming more crowded. Meaning customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.720
acquisition costs were going up.
But Squarespace had a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.098
problem to everyone else.
Squarespace had expanded into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.098 --> 00:12:20.000
ecommerce, scheduling, restaurant technology,&nbsp;
payments, enterprise, domains, and so much more.&nbsp;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.680
I’m sure you know the phrase “Jack of all trades”…
Well, Squarespace was the master of none.&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:30.880
Not as commerce-focused as Shopify. Not&nbsp;
as open and extensible as WordPress.&nbsp;

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:35.440
Plus other acquisitions, like Google&nbsp;
Domains, didn’t really help with growth.&nbsp;

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:39.920
A domain customer… isn’t&nbsp;
necessarily a new website customer.&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:44.240
And the biggest case was Tock.
Not only did it have a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:48.400
write-down, Squarespace sold Tock to&nbsp;
American Express for $400 million.&nbsp;

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:55.440
That might not sound like a big deal. Bought&nbsp;
for 400 mil, sold for 400 mil… but it is.&nbsp;

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.160
It means it wasn’t actually&nbsp;
adding value to Squarespace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.800
and as an asset, it wasn’t appreciating.
That cash could have gone somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.920
which did make a return.
And all this came to a head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:12.320
when Squarespace finally looked to leave the NYSE.
But it wasn’t delisting, it was a buyout.&nbsp;

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:16.160
This put Anthony Casalena at&nbsp;
odds with the other shareholders.&nbsp;

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:21.200
In May 2024, Squarespace agreed to be&nbsp;
acquired by UK private equity firm Permira,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:25.920
in an all-cash deal valued at $6.9 billion.
“Squarespace stockholders will receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.680
$44.00 per share in cash”
Casalena actually had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.880
meeting with Permira as far back as late 2022
and he agreed to roll over most of his equity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.520
and stay on as CEO and board chairman.
Sounds like a good exit… but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:44.080
shareholders weren’t exactly thrilled.
Casalena would remain one of the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:48.960
shareholders, while minority shareholders got&nbsp;
a fixed cash price, without much say in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:55.280
of it. And not a particularly great cash price.
Plus, while Squarespace approached a few private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:01.680
equity firms, other companies weren’t approached&nbsp;
at all, because Casalena wanted to stay in charge.&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.840
“Despite the offer representing a premium at&nbsp;
announcement to historic trading levels and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.880
valuation multiples, there appears to&nbsp;
be no downside risk of non-approval”.&nbsp;

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.880
Glazer Capital, LLC, one of the biggest minority&nbsp;
Squarespace shareholders, also published an open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.520
letter to the board of directors.
[Glazer Capital, LLC]: “We strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:24.160
believe that the Merger Consideration is&nbsp;
demonstrably inadequate and is the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:29.600
of a self-serving go-private process engineered&nbsp;
by a founder-CEO that presently controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:35.360
and desires to retain control over, Squarespace's&nbsp;
destiny. It is also our view that the Acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:40.480
was orchestrated to deliver the Company into the&nbsp;
arms of a handpicked private equity consortium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:45.120
Accordingly, we intend to vote all of our&nbsp;
shares against this transaction at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:51.520
upcoming meeting of Squarespace shareholders.”
Permira returned with a revised offer. $46.50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:57.360
per share, an increase of $2.50. But also,&nbsp;
a change to the structure of the deal.&nbsp;

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:01.680
In the first offer, Squarespace needed a&nbsp;
shareholder vote, which was mostly controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:07.040
by a couple of individual investors and Casalena.&nbsp;
This time, Permira just offered to buy shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:13.760
directly. Once enough shareholders sold, the&nbsp;
rest were bought out automatically, at $46.50.&nbsp;

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:19.120
And this time, it went through.
Squarespace, was now private again.&nbsp;

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:23.760
Squarespace is still around today,&nbsp;
you may even see it in a sponsorship.&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:29.120
But growth has slowed dramatically.
Now, they’re competing against a new competitor.&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:35.760
Not so much Wix or WordPress, but AI.
Vibe-coded websites. No need for templates.&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:41.360
Now they’re investing into R&amp;D and AI to keep up.
But Squarespace’s best chance against this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:46.480
is their most valuable asset: their brand.
That high quality association they’ve spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.400
years building, across so many&nbsp;
sponsorships and Super Bowls.&nbsp;

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:55.040
But this is an ongoing battle.
Only time will tell if Squarespace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.960
can survive this new opponent.
Their long-term rival, Wix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:04.480
is also wrestling with these challenges.
But, they don’t have quite the same beloved brand.&nbsp;

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:08.160
In fact, some of their marketing&nbsp;
led them to annoy customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:13.920
and into some weird drama with WordPress.
